(6716075, 1003712, 46202, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by wwjdwithca:</b></div><hr/><i> This was my favorite image in the contest, the composition, the colors and the lighting setup.  How did you do the lighting by the way?  Thanks for the effort, it''s not that I don''t love shots of pickles on a table (that normally win these contests), but I do really enjoy these types of pictures.  There is so much work involved, yet it seems to go unnoticed.  One requirement to me is, "Would I put this on my wall?", and this clearly meets that!  Nice work. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks so much!  I used an SB-600 flash (on a stand just off to camera right) triggered by a Pocket Wizard transceiver.  I also velcro''d a warm toned gel over the flash to give it a warmer cast and the set the person apart a little more from the background.  :o)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2012-03-19 11:20:03.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by wwjdwithca: This was my favorite image in the contest, the composition, the colors and the lighting setup.  How did you do the lighting by the way?  Thanks for the effort, it''s not that I don''t love shots of pickles on a table (that normally win these contests), but I do really enjoy these types of pictures.  There is so much work involved, yet it seems to go unnoticed.  One requirement to me is, "Would I put this on my wall?", and this clearly meets that!  Nice work.   Thanks so much!  I used an SB-600 flash (on a stand just off to camera right) triggered by a Pocket Wizard transceiver.  I also velcro''d a warm toned gel over the flash to give it a warmer cast and the set the person apart a little more from the background.  :o)', '2012-03-19 10:31:19.000000', '2012-03-19 11:20:03.000000', 0),
